(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-15"] = {"chem-12-15-m01":{"idea":"A polymer is a giant molecule built from many small repeating units.","steps":["Poly means many, and mer means part.","Small units called monomers join one after another.","The result is a molecule of very large mass.","Plastics, fibres and rubbers are all polymers."],"others":"One-atom molecules of equal size are not polymers. A fixed ratio of two atoms says nothing about size. Polymers are made by joining small units, not by splitting large ones.","trap":"Polymer = many units joined, never split.","answer":"has very large molecules, made by joining many small units"},"chem-12-15-m02":{"idea":"Polythene is an addition polymer of a molecule with a C=C double bond.","steps":["The monomer must have a double bond to open and join.","Ethene, CH₂=CH₂, has one.","n CH₂=CH₂ → (–CH₂–CH₂–)ₙ.","This chain is polythene."],"others":"Ethane has no double bond, so it cannot add on. Ethyne has a triple bond and does not give polythene. Propene gives polypropene.","trap":"Polythene from ethene, polypropene from propene.","answer":"ethene, CH₂=CH₂"},"chem-12-15-m03":{"idea":"PVC stands for polyvinyl chloride, so the monomer is vinyl chloride.","steps":["Vinyl chloride is CH₂=CHCl, which has a double bond.","n CH₂=CHCl → (–CH₂–CHCl–)ₙ.","The polymer is PVC.","It is used for pipes and cables."],"others":"Ethyl chloride has no double bond. Chlorobenzene is a ring compound and does not add on this way. Vinylidene fluoride is the monomer of a different polymer.","trap":"Name check: polyvinyl chloride = poly(vinyl chloride).","answer":"vinyl chloride, CH₂=CHCl"},"chem-12-15-m04":{"idea":"Teflon is a polymer in which every hydrogen of polythene is replaced by fluorine.","steps":["The monomer is tetrafluoroethene, CF₂=CF₂.","n CF₂=CF₂ → (–CF₂–CF₂–)ₙ.","C–F bonds are very strong.","So Teflon is inert and slippery, and is used on non-stick pans."],"others":"Trifluoroethene has only three fluorines and is not the monomer of Teflon. Propene gives polypropene. Chloroethene gives PVC.","trap":"Tetra = four fluorines.","answer":"tetrafluoroethene, CF₂=CF₂"},"chem-12-15-m05":{"idea":"Addition polymers lose nothing when they form, and their monomers have a double bond.","steps":["Monomers with a C=C bond add to each other.","No atom is left out.","So the polymer has the same atoms as the monomers together.","Examples are polythene, PVC and Teflon."],"others":"Losing HCl or water happens in condensation polymerisation. A monomer with two functional groups is typical of condensation, not addition.","trap":"Addition: double bond, nothing lost.","answer":"no small molecule is lost, and the monomers have a double bond"},"chem-12-15-m06":{"idea":"Condensation polymers lose a small molecule at every joining step.","steps":["The monomers have two functional groups each.","When two groups react, a small molecule leaves, for example H₂O.","This happens at every link.","Nylon-6,6, terylene and Bakelite form this way."],"others":"Giving out only heat does not describe condensation. Nothing is lost in addition polymerisation. Oxygen is not taken in during polymer formation.","trap":"Condensation = small molecule lost.","answer":"a small molecule such as water is lost at each step"},"chem-12-15-m07":{"idea":"Nylon-6,6 is made from a six-carbon diamine and a six-carbon diacid.","steps":["Hexamethylenediamine is H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂, with six carbons.","Adipic acid is HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH, with six carbons.","They join by amide links and water is lost.","The two sixes give the name 6,6."],"others":"Phenol with formaldehyde gives Bakelite. Caprolactam is used for nylon-6. Ethylene glycol with terephthalic acid gives terylene.","trap":"6,6 = diamine with 6 C, diacid with 6 C.","answer":"hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid"},"chem-12-15-m08":{"idea":"Nylon-6 forms from a ring compound that opens and joins.","steps":["Caprolactam is a six-carbon ring with an amide group.","Heating with water opens the ring to give an amino acid.","The amino acid units join with loss of water.","The product is (–NH–(CH₂)₅–CO–)ₙ."],"others":"Sulfur cross-links make vulcanised rubber. Adding to ethene gives an addition polymer, not nylon. Adipic acid is used for nylon-6,6.","trap":"Nylon-6 uses one monomer; nylon-6,6 uses two.","answer":"heating it with water, which opens the ring and joins the units"},"chem-12-15-m09":{"idea":"Terylene is a polyester, so it needs an alcohol and an acid, each with two groups.","steps":["Ethylene glycol has two OH groups.","Terephthalic acid has two COOH groups.","OH and COOH join by ester links and water is lost.","The long chain is terylene, used for clothes."],"others":"A diamine with adipic acid gives nylon-6,6. Butadiene with styrene gives Buna-S. Phenol with formaldehyde gives Bakelite.","trap":"Ester link = polyester.","answer":"ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid"},"chem-12-15-m10":{"idea":"A thermosetting polymer has cross-links, so it sets hard and does not soften again.","steps":["Phenol and formaldehyde form a three-dimensional network.","Chains are linked by cross-links.","Heat cannot soften it again.","So Bakelite is a thermosetting polymer."],"others":"An elastomer is rubber-like and stretchy. Bakelite is not spun into threads, so it is not a fibre. A thermoplastic softens again on heating, which Bakelite does not.","trap":"Thermoplastic = softens again. Thermosetting = sets for ever.","answer":"a thermosetting polymer, which cannot be softened again by heating"},"chem-12-15-m11":{"idea":"Natural rubber is a polymer of one diene.","steps":["Natural rubber is cis-1,4-polyisoprene.","Its monomer is isoprene, 2-methylbuta-1,3-diene.","Each unit keeps a double bond in the chain.","The cis shape makes the chains coil and gives elasticity."],"others":"Styrene is used for polystyrene and Buna-S. Chloroprene gives neoprene. Butadiene lacks the methyl group of isoprene.","trap":"Isoprene has a methyl group on C-2.","answer":"isoprene (2-methylbuta-1,3-diene)"},"chem-12-15-m12":{"idea":"Vulcanisation adds sulfur cross-links between rubber chains.","steps":["Sulfur reacts at the double bonds of the chains.","It forms bridges between neighbouring chains.","The chains cannot slip past each other.","So the rubber is harder, stronger and returns to shape after stretching."],"others":"Sulfur does not remove all the double bonds. It does not leave elasticity unchanged. It does not cut chains, so it does not make rubber sticky.","trap":"Cross-links give strength and elasticity.","answer":"harder and more elastic, because sulfur makes cross-links between the chains"},"chem-12-15-m13":{"idea":"The S in Buna-S stands for styrene.","steps":["Buna-S is a synthetic rubber.","It is a copolymer: two monomers are used.","The monomers are buta-1,3-diene and styrene.","It is used in tyres."],"others":"Ethene with propene is not Buna-S. Butadiene with acrylonitrile is Buna-N. Phenol with formaldehyde gives Bakelite.","trap":"S = styrene, N = nitrile.","answer":"buta-1,3-diene and styrene"},"chem-12-15-m14":{"idea":"The N in Buna-N stands for nitrile.","steps":["Buna-N is a copolymer of buta-1,3-diene and acrylonitrile.","Acrylonitrile is CH₂=CH–CN, a nitrile.","The rubber resists oils and solvents.","It is used for oil seals and hoses."],"others":"Ethene with vinyl chloride is not Buna-N. Butadiene with styrene is Buna-S. Isoprene with sulfur is not a monomer pair for this rubber.","trap":"N = nitrile, S = styrene.","answer":"buta-1,3-diene and acrylonitrile"},"chem-12-15-m15":{"idea":"Thermoplastics soften when heated and can be moulded again and again.","steps":["Their chains are linear or slightly branched.","The forces between chains are weak.","Heat softens them and cooling hardens them.","Polythene and PVC are examples."],"others":"Stretching and returning to shape describes an elastomer. Burning with a carbon residue is not the definition. Setting for ever on heating is a thermosetting polymer.","trap":"Thermo + plastic = softens with heat.","answer":"softens on heating and hardens on cooling, and can be moulded again"},"chem-12-15-m16":{"idea":"Fibres need strong forces between chains so that threads can be drawn.","steps":["Nylon chains have N–H and C=O groups.","These make hydrogen bonds between chains.","Strong forces give high tensile strength.","So fibres can be drawn into strong threads."],"others":"A fibre is not a network and it has forces. Ionic bonds do not join nylon chains. Very weak forces that let chains slide freely describe elastomers.","trap":"Fibre = strong forces. Elastomer = weak forces.","answer":"strong forces such as hydrogen bonds between the chains"},"chem-12-15-m17":{"idea":"A copolymer is made from two or more different monomers.","steps":["A homopolymer has one kind of monomer.","Polythene, PVC and Teflon each have one monomer.","Buna-S has butadiene and styrene.","So Buna-S is the copolymer."],"others":"Polythene, PVC and Teflon are each made from one monomer only, so the reason given in each is false.","trap":"Co = together; more than one monomer.","answer":"Buna-S, as it has two different monomers"},"chem-12-15-m18":{"idea":"A cross-linked polymer has covalent bridges between chains, making a network.","steps":["Covalent bonds join chain to chain in three dimensions.","The chains cannot move apart.","So the polymer is hard and rigid.","Bakelite is an example."],"others":"A linear polymer has no network. A branched polymer is not the one with covalent links between chains. Cross-linked polymers are not soft and rubbery.","trap":"Cross-linked = hard, rigid, not easily melted.","answer":"a cross-linked polymer, which is hard and rigid"},"chem-12-15-m19":{"idea":"Degree of polymerisation counts how many monomer units are in one chain.","steps":["It is the number of repeating units per polymer molecule.","DP ≈ M / m, where M is the polymer molar mass and m is the mass of one unit.","Chains differ in length, so DP is an average."],"others":"It is not the percentage converted, not the mass in grams, and not the number of different monomers.","trap":"DP is size by count, not by mass.","answer":"the number of monomer units in one polymer molecule"},"chem-12-15-m20":{"idea":"Polymer chains in a sample differ in length, so only an average mass makes sense.","steps":["Chains stop growing at random.","So a sample is a mixture of chains with different numbers of units.","Their masses differ.","We therefore quote an average molar mass."],"others":"Temperature does not change the mass of a chain. The monomers in a sample have the same mass. Chains are not all the same length.","trap":"Average because the chains are unequal.","answer":"the sample has chains with many different lengths"},"chem-12-15-m21":{"idea":"Hydrogen bonds between nylon chains add strength, so shorter chains are enough.","steps":["Nylon has N–H and C=O groups.","They form hydrogen bonds between chains.","These bonds add to the strength.","Polythene has only weak forces and needs longer chains.","So nylon needs a smaller critical degree of polymerisation."],"others":"Nylon-6 is not cross-linked with sulfur. Polythene has weaker forces, not stronger ones. Mass per unit does not set the strength.","trap":"Stronger forces mean shorter chains are enough.","answer":"hydrogen bonds between the chains of nylon add to the strength"},"chem-12-15-m22":{"idea":"PHBV is useful in medicine and packaging because microorganisms can break it down.","steps":["PHBV is a polyester.","Microbes can break its ester links.","It turns into carbon dioxide and water.","So it does not stay in the environment or body."],"others":"It is not non-biodegradable. It is not a thermoset. It is not a conductor of current.","trap":"PHBV = biodegradable polyester.","answer":"is biodegradable, as microorganisms can break it down"},"chem-12-15-m23":{"idea":"PHBV has ester links, and enzymes of microbes can break those links.","steps":["PHBV is a polyester.","Microbes make enzymes called esterases.","They hydrolyse the ester links.","Polythene has only C–C bonds that microbes cannot attack."],"others":"PHBV does have breakable links. Sunlight alone does not break its carbon chain. It has ester links, not amide links.","trap":"Ester links are the weak point.","answer":"its ester links can be broken by enzymes of microorganisms"},"chem-12-15-m24":{"idea":"Nylon-2-nylon-6 is a copolymer of two amino acids.","steps":["One monomer is glycine, H₂N–CH₂–COOH.","The other is aminocaproic acid, H₂N–(CH₂)₅–COOH.","They join by amide links.","Microbes can hydrolyse the amide links, so it is biodegradable."],"others":"Adipic acid with the diamine gives nylon-6,6. Butadiene with acrylonitrile gives Buna-N. Glycol with terephthalic acid gives terylene.","trap":"2 and 6 count carbons in the two amino acids.","answer":"glycine and aminocaproic acid"},"chem-12-15-m25":{"idea":"Condensation polymers lose a small molecule such as water as they form.","steps":["Nylon-6,6 forms from a diamine and a diacid.","Water is lost at each amide link.","Polythene, PVC and Teflon form from C=C monomers.","They lose nothing, so they are addition polymers."],"others":"Water is not lost when polythene, PVC or Teflon forms. They are addition polymers.","trap":"Water lost = condensation.","answer":"nylon-6,6, as water is lost when it forms"},"chem-12-15-n01":{"idea":"Degree of polymerisation is the polymer molar mass divided by the mass of one unit.","steps":["DP = M / m.","M = 28 000 g/mol and m = 28 g/mol for ethene.","DP = 28 000 / 28 = 1000."],"trap":"Check: 1000 units × 28 = 28 000.","answer":"1000"},"chem-12-15-n02":{"idea":"Number average molar mass weights each chain type by how many chains there are.","steps":["M_n = ΣNᵢMᵢ / ΣNᵢ.","Numerator = 2.0 × 20 000 + 3.0 × 40 000 = 160 000.","Denominator = 2.0 + 3.0 = 5.0.","M_n = 160 000 / 5.0 = 32 000 g/mol."],"trap":"Check: total mass 160 000 g over 5.0 mol of chains.","answer":"32000 g/mol"},"chem-12-15-n03":{"idea":"Mass average molar mass gives heavier chains more weight.","steps":["M_w = ΣNᵢMᵢ² / ΣNᵢMᵢ.","Numerator = 2.0 × (2 × 10⁴)² + 3.0 × (4 × 10⁴)² = 8 × 10⁸ + 4.8 × 10⁹ = 5.6 × 10⁹.","Denominator = 160 000 = 1.6 × 10⁵.","M_w = 5.6 × 10⁹ / 1.6 × 10⁵ = 35 000 g/mol."],"trap":"M_w is larger than M_n because heavy chains count more.","answer":"35000 g/mol"},"chem-12-15-n04":{"idea":"Polydispersity index shows how wide the spread of chain lengths is.","steps":["PDI = M_w / M_n.","M_w = 35 000 and M_n = 32 000 g/mol.","PDI = 35 000 / 32 000 = 1.094.","To 2 decimal places, 1.09."],"trap":"PDI = 1 would mean all chains are equal in length.","answer":"1.09"},"chem-12-15-n05":{"idea":"Find the mass of one repeating unit, then use moles of adipic acid.","steps":["Repeating unit = 146 + 116 − 2 × 18 = 226 g/mol.","Moles of adipic acid = 14.6 / 146 = 0.100 mol.","Moles of units = 0.100 mol.","Mass = 0.100 × 226 = 22.6 g."],"trap":"Check: C₁₂H₂₂N₂O₂ = 144 + 22 + 28 + 32 = 226.","answer":"22.6 g"},"chem-12-15-n06":{"idea":"Find the mass of one terylene unit, then use moles of terephthalic acid.","steps":["Repeating unit = 166 + 62 − 2 × 18 = 192 g/mol.","Moles of acid = 16.6 / 166 = 0.100 mol.","Mass of polymer = 0.100 × 192 = 19.2 g."],"trap":"Check: C₁₀H₈O₄ = 120 + 8 + 64 = 192.","answer":"19.2 g"},"chem-12-15-n07":{"idea":"In addition polymerisation, the polymer mass equals the monomer mass that reacted.","steps":["Nothing is lost, so mass of polymer = mass of monomer reacted.","Reacted mass = 31.25 × 0.80.","= 25.0 g."],"trap":"Check: 31.25 g is 0.500 mol; 0.400 mol × 62.5 = 25.0 g.","answer":"25.0 g"},"chem-12-15-n08":{"idea":"Mass percentage = mass of styrene part / total mass of the group × 100.","steps":["One group has 3 butadiene and 1 styrene unit.","Mass = 3 × 54 + 104 = 266 g/mol.","Styrene part = 104 / 266 × 100.","= 39.10 percent."],"trap":"Check: 1000 units as 750 butadiene and 250 styrene give the same ratio.","answer":"39.10 %"},"chem-12-15-n09":{"idea":"Mass percentage of an element = its mass in one unit / unit mass × 100.","steps":["The repeating unit is C₂H₃Cl.","Mass = 24 + 3 + 35.5 = 62.5 g/mol.","Cl percentage = 35.5 / 62.5 × 100.","= 56.8 percent."],"trap":"Check: 35 500 g of Cl in 62 500 g of 1000 units.","answer":"56.8 %"}};
